<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adolphe Thiers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adolphe Thiers was a French historian and statesman who served as a minister and prime minister under the July Monarchy. After the defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> he became head of the executive in 1871, made peace and sent the army to crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> in May 1871. As first president of the Third Republic he paid off the war indemnity before resigning in 1873.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adolphe-thiers.jpg" alt="Adolphe Thiers" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adolphe_Thiers_Nadar_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adolphe Thiers was born out of wedlock in Marseille on 15 April 1797, during the rule of the Directorate. His father, Pierre-Louis-Marie Thiers, a businessman and occasional government official under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, married his mother, Marie-Madeleine Amic, on 13 May 1797, which made Adolphe legitimate, but he soon disappeared. He contacted his son only in 1825, when Adolphe was becoming well known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> journalism, to ask for money. Thiers replied that his only loyalty was to his mother, who had raised him alone, though he later supported his father and half-brothers. On his mother's side he had Levantine ancestry: his maternal grandfather Claude Amic had worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and married a Greek-speaking woman there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Thiers was a key figure in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> of 1830, which replaced Charles X with Louis Philippe, and in the Revolution of 1848, which ended the July Monarchy. He was prime minister in 1836 and 1840, dedicated the Arc de Triomphe, and arranged the return of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s remains from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a>. He was first a supporter and then a vocal opponent of Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte, and was arrested and briefly expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> seized power.</p><p>Thiers opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s defeat he was elected chief executive and negotiated its end. In March 1871 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> seized power, and he gave the army the orders to suppress it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> named him president of the Republic in August 1871, when he was seventy-four; his chief accomplishment was the departure of German soldiers from most of French territory two years ahead of schedule. Opposed by monarchists and by the republican left, he resigned on 24 May 1873. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-de-macmahon">Patrice de MacMahon</a> replaced him. He died on 3 September 1877, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> and Léon Gambetta led the procession at his funeral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Thiers was also a popular historian. His ten-volume history of the French Revolution, published from 1823 to 1827, was the first large-scale history of it in French. It condemned the Terror and radical leaders such as Marat and Robespierre and praised moderates such as Mirabeau and Lafayette, and it did much to undermine support for the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king. He followed it with a twenty-volume Histoire du Consulat et de l'Empire, helped by his access to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s surviving generals. He was elected to the Académie Française in 1834.</p><p>He built the ring of fortifications around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> known as the Thiers Wall, whose line now marks the city limits, and completed the column on the Place Vendôme. Judgements of him depended on the politics of those judging him: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> attacked him in 1871, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustave-flaubert">Gustave Flaubert</a> wrote that no one had summed up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> better.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adolphe Thiers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the surrender of January 1871, a conservative National Assembly made <b>Adolphe Thiers</b> head of the executive and signed the Treaty of Frankfurt on 10 May 1871, ceding Alsace and most of Lorraine to the new German Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Rhine Crisis of 1840 the French prime minister <b>Adolphe Thiers</b> claimed the Upper and Middle Rhine as France's &quot;natural eastern border&quot;, setting off a movement of Rhine songs in Germany.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a></li><li><blockquote>Government rested on a narrow electorate of wealthy men, and ministers such as François Guizot and <b>Adolphe Thiers</b> alternated in power.</blockquote><a href="/state/july-monarchy">July Monarchy</a></li><li><blockquote>After France's defeat by Prussia and the long siege of Paris, the government of <b>Adolphe Thiers</b> tried to remove the National Guard's cannons from Montmartre on 18 March 1871.</blockquote><a href="/state/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Marseille, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Saint-Germain-en-Laye, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.3,5.38&amp;year=1849" aria-label="Marseille on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4680 628 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adolphe Thiers"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5130 898h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5130 898h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.3,5.38&amp;year=1849">What was here in 1849? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
